.\" This is a simple manual page for printf function
.TH _PRINTF 3 "18-11-2025" "printf project" "Basic library function"
.SH NAME
_Printf \- writes a formatted output
.SH LIBRARY
Not part of standard C library
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include "main.h"
.PP
.BI "int _printf(const char *" format ", ...);"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B _printf()
function is a simple  function that tries to act like the standard
.BR printf (3)
function, but in a smaller and simpler way. It is part of the printf project 
and it is not the real function from the C library. It sends output
to the standard output (stdout) and provides the number of characters
that it printed.
.PP
The first argument to
.B _printf()
is a format string. This format string can contain normal characters
and also special characters that start with a percent sign (%). The
normal characters are printed exactly as they are. When the function
finds a percent sign, it looks at the next character to decide how to
handle it and which extra argument to read.
.PP
.B _printf()
can also take unlimited number of arguments with different data types by using the
.B variadic() 
function. In each call,
.B _printf()
uses 
.B variadic()
function to deal with different numbers of arguments. It creates
.BR va_list ,
to read these arguments,
and
.BI va_start (args, format)
to initialize it. Then it uses
.BI va_arg (args, type)
each time to get the next value. When all the arguments finished, it calls
.BI va_end (args)
to clean up.
.PP
The
.B _printf()
does not print everything by itself. It goes through the format string
one character at a time. When it sees a normal character, it sends it
directly to
.BR _putchar()
and increases an internal counter that tracks the total number of characters printed.
When it sees a percent sign (%), it looks at the next
character and chooses a suitable helper function such as
.BR print_char() ,
.BR print_string() ,
.BR print_percent()
or
.BR print_int()
depending on the conversion specifier. Each helper function prints
its part of the output using
.BR _putchar()
and returns the count of characters written, and then
.B _printf()
adds this number to the total count.
.PP
This basic 
.B _printf()
function only supports a small set of conversion
specifiers. Anything else starting with % is not supported and
.B _printf()
will just print the percent sign and the next character.
.PP
The supported conversion specifiers are:
.TP
.B %c
Prints a single character. The corresponding argument is taken as an
.IR int ,
and it is converted to char before printing. Only one
character is printed.
.TP
.B %s
Prints a string of characters that ends with ('\\0'). The
corresponding argument is a pointer to
.IR char .
The function prints the characters in the string one by one until it
reaches the end of the string. If the pointer is
.BR NULL ,
the function prints "(null)".
.TP
.B %%
Prints a single percent sign. This is useful when the user wants to print a %
character.
.TP
.B %d
Prints a signed decimal integer. The corresponding argument is of type
.IR int .
If the number is negative, the function first prints a minus sign and
then prints the absolute value of the number as digits from 0 to 9.
.TP
.B %i
Behaves the same as
.BR %d .
It also prints a signed decimal integer using an
.IR int
argument.
.PP
If the character after the percent sign is not one of the supported
specifiers, the function prints the percent sign and that character as they are. 
This means that a format like "%q" will be printed as "%q" in the
output.
.PP
The 
.B _printf()
function does not support specifiers like field width, precision or length
modifiers as they are not required for this project.
Only the basic forms
.BR %c ,
.BR %s ,
.BR %% ,
.BR %d
and
.BR %i
are supported.
.PP
The
.B _printf()
function uses
.BR _putchar()
to send one character at a time to the 
.IR stdout . 
The
.B _putchar()
function calls the
.BR write (2)
system call with 
.IR stdout . 
The characters printed by the helper functions are counted, 
and the total count is returned.
If the
.I format
pointer is
.BR NULL ,
or if a percent sign appears at the end of the format string
without a valid specifier after it, the function does not print a
normal result and returns
.BR -1
to indicate an error. In all other supported cases it returns the
number of characters that were actually printed.
.SH RETURN VALUE
Upon successful, 
.B _printf()
returns the number of bytes printed 
(excluding the null byte used to end output to strings).
.PP
On error, a negative value is returned, and
.B -1 
is set to indicate the error.
.SH ERRORS
The following errors may occur:
.IP \[bu] 2
The
.I format
pointer is
.BR NULL .
.IP \[bu] 2
A percent character ('%') appears at the end of the format
string without a following conversion specifier.
.SH STANDARDS
The
.B _printf()
function is specific for printf project and is not part of
any standard.
.SH HISTORY
The first version of
.B _printf()
function was developed according to task 0 requirements of printf project.
It supports the basic conversion specifiers (%c, %s and %%).
.PP
The second version of
.B _printf()
function was updated according to task 1 requirements to support integers using the %d and %i specifiers.
.SH CAVEATS
.BR _printf()
does not support all format specifiers that are supported by real
.BR printf (3)
function such as %f, %x, %u, and %p.
When unsupported conversion specifiers passed to
.BR _printf() 
the percent sign and the following character will be printed as it is,
which means the program will work without an error but will not give the expected output.
.SH BUGS
.BR _printf() 
has not been tested for possible bugs.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
#include "main.h"
int main(void)
{
    _printf("Single character: %c\\n", 'C');
    _printf("Massage: %s\\n", "Hello, World");
    _printf("Percent sign: %%\\n");
    _printf("Number: %d\\n", 1911);
    _printf("Signed number: %i\\n", -25);
    return (0);
}
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR printf (3),
.BR write (2),
.BR stdarg (3)
